140k  That was the massive amount.  These days it's hard to get an image resource to be below 140K.  But at the time, it was a MASSIVE amount of storage.  You could keep DOZENS of programs on a disk.\
\
The Apple series had two disk operating systems - DOS 3.3 (16 256 byte sectors, 35 tracks), and ProDOS. ProDOS came later and was aimed for hard drives (starting at a massive of five megabytes), having a hierarchical file system.  For folks just using floppies, it was big, consumed a ton of memory, and 5 1/4" floppies are not really amenable to subdirectories and paths.\
\
I'm going to be using DOS 3.3 and ignoring ProDOS.  If you're running a IIgs, you'll want to look for something that talks about ProDOS.\
